    "## 3. Understand rows, columns and data types\n",
    "\n",
    "The expected dataset has 2,000 records, 20 predictor columns and one target column (`price_range`). Verify this from the loaded file rather than assuming it."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "id": "1e56e5b1",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "print(\"Rows:\", df.shape[0])\n",
    "print(\"Columns:\", df.shape[1])\n",
    "print(\"\\\\nColumn names:\")\n",
    "print(df.columns.tolist())\n",
    "\n",
    "print(\"\\\\nDataFrame information:\")\n",
    "df.info()\n",
    "\n",
    "display(df.describe().T)"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "626bbe73",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## 4. Check missing values and duplicate rows\n",
    "\n",
    "Missing values must be handled in the preprocessing stage. Do not fill them using statistics calculated from the complete dataset before splitting. The preprocessing pipeline should learn imputation values from training data only."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "id": "30100222",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "missing = df.isna().sum().sort_values(ascending=False)\n",
    "missing = missing[missing > 0]\n",
    "\n",
    "print(\"Missing values by column:\")\n",
    "display(missing.to_frame(\"missing_count\"))\n",
    "\n",
    "print(\"Total missing cells:\", int(df.isna().sum().sum()))\n",
    "print(\"Duplicate rows:\", int(df.duplicated().sum()))"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "06c57a82",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## 5. Inspect target distribution\n",
    "\n",
    "A balanced target means each class has a similar number of observations. This helps us decide whether class weighting or resampling is necessary. The target should be kept as a four-class categorical outcome."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "id": "6a52da0f",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "target_labels = {\n",
    "    0: \"Low cost\",\n",
    "    1: \"Medium cost\",\n",
    "    2: \"High cost\",\n",
    "    3: \"Very high cost\",\n",
    "}\n",
    "\n",
    "target_counts = df[\"price_range\"].value_counts().sort_index()\n",
    "target_table = pd.DataFrame({\n",
    "    \"class_label\": [target_labels.get(i, str(i)) for i in target_counts.index],\n",
    "    \"count\": target_counts.values,\n",
    "    \"percentage\": (target_counts.values / len(df) * 100).round(2),\n",
    "})\n",
    "display(target_table)\n",
    "\n",
    "ax = sns.countplot(data=df, x=\"price_range\", order=[0, 1, 2, 3])\n",
    "ax.set_title(\"Distribution of Mobile Phone Price Categories\")\n",
    "ax.set_xlabel(\"Price category (0 = Low, 3 = Very high)\")\n",
    "ax.set_ylabel(\"Number of phones\")\n",
    "plt.tight_layout()\n",
    "plt.show()"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "0d068dcf",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## 6. Compare important numerical features across price categories\n",
    "\n",
    "These plots are exploratory, not proof of causation. Look for features whose distributions change noticeably across the four classes. Final feature selection will be performed later using training data only."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "id": "e45b29fc",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "candidate_features = [\n",
    "    \"ram\", \"battery_power\", \"px_height\", \"px_width\",\n",
    "    \"mobile_wt\", \"int_memory\", \"clock_speed\"\n",
    "]\n",
    "available_features = [c for c in candidate_features if c in df.columns]\n",
    "\n",
    "fig, axes = plt.subplots(len(available_features), 1, figsize=(10, 4 * len(available_features)))\n",
    "if len(available_features) == 1:\n",
    "    axes = [axes]\n",
    "\n",
    "for ax, feature in zip(axes, available_features):\n",
    "    sns.boxplot(data=df, x=\"price_range\", y=feature, order=[0, 1, 2, 3], ax=ax)\n",
    "    ax.set_title(f\"{feature} by price category\")\n",
    "    ax.set_xlabel(\"Price category\")\n",
    "    ax.set_ylabel(feature)\n",
    "\n",
    "plt.tight_layout()\n",
    "plt.show()"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "b44bb963",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## 7. Correlation analysis\n",
    "\n",
    "The Pearson correlation with `price_range` is a preliminary linear association check. Since the target classes are ordered integers, interpret these values cautiously. Correlation alone will not determine the final feature subset."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "id": "755a778d",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "numeric_df = df.select_dtypes(include=np.number)\n",
    "corr = numeric_df.corr()\n",
    "\n",
    "plt.figure(figsize=(15, 12))\n",
    "sns.heatmap(corr, cmap=\"coolwarm\", center=0, annot=False, linewidths=0.2)\n",
    "plt.title(\"Correlation Heatmap of Numerical Variables\")\n",
    "plt.tight_layout()\n",
    "plt.show()\n",
    "\n",
    "target_corr = corr[\"price_range\"].drop(\"price_range\").sort_values(\n",
    "    key=lambda s: s.abs(), ascending=False\n",
    ")\n",
    "display(target_corr.to_frame(\"correlation_with_price_range\"))"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "b50e82d3",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## 8. EDA findings and handover to Member 2\n",
    "\n",
    "Record findings from the actual outputs above. For the supplied dataset, the initial inspection found:\n",
    "\n",
    "- 2,000 rows and 21 columns.\n",
    "- `price_range` is present and contains four classes (0, 1, 2, 3).\n",
    "- The four target classes each contain 500 records (25% each).\n",
    "- Five missing cells are present: one each in `int_memory`, `m_dep`, `px_width`, `ram`, and `three_g`.\n",
    "- No duplicate rows were found.\n",
    "- `ram` has the strongest positive Pearson correlation with `price_range` in this dataset; `battery_power`, `px_width`, and `px_height` also show positive associations. This is exploratory evidence only, not the final feature selection.\n",
    "\n",
    "### Handover notes\n",
    "1. Member 2 should create a stratified train/validation/test split before fitting imputers, scalers or feature selectors.\n",
    "2. Use the training partition only to fit preprocessing and feature-selection steps.\n",
    "3. Compare candidate feature counts using cross-validation on training data, then check the chosen configuration on validation data.\n",
    "4. Keep the test set untouched until final evaluation by Member 3.\n",
    "5. Because the target classes are balanced, do not apply oversampling or undersampling unless later evidence justifies it.\n",
    "\n",
    "**End of Member 1 EDA notebook.**"
   ]
  }
